We have the cxx_python_rules stored in a separate repository, and can pull from
it using git subtree (note this only matters for a developer, a user doesn't
need to know about subtree).

The subtree was added as swig_rules (repository swig-rules) and renamed to
cxx_python_rules (repository cxx-python-rules) in 2026-11. The subtree
commands below look for the history under the prefix they are given, so
the first sync after the rename has to be done by diff and copy (or a new
"git subtree add") before they work with --prefix cxx_python_rules.

Note that git subtree isn't installed by default with git, however it is a
simple install from the git source tree, found in the contrib directory.

If the git repository is added as the remote cxx-python-rules-repo, then we pull
in the latest changes by:

git subtree pull --prefix cxx_python_rules cxx-python-rules-repo master
(note we can include "--squash" here if we want to put the whole history
in as one pull).

Changes can be pushed up stream by:

git subtree push --prefix cxx_python_rules cxx-python-rules-repo master

We can look at differences with upstream:

git fetch cxx-python-rules-repo
git diff HEAD:cxx_python_rules cxx-python-rules-repo/master

Note if you need to add in a new subtree, the initial creation is by:
git subtree add --prefix cxx_python_rules cxx-python-rules-repo master

Sometimes you will get errors saying "Updates were rejected because the
tip of current branch is behind." First do a subtree pull if you haven't,
this may be real. But you might get this even if a pull says everything
is up to date. This appears to be a bug in subtree, see description at
http://stackoverflow.com/questions/13756055/git-subtree-subtree-up-to-date-but-cant-push

Can try 
git push cxx-python-rules-repo `git subtree split --prefix cxx_python_rules master`:master --force
